{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And you shall bring back to your heart.</b> This is a positive commandment from the Torah — the knowledge of HaShem, blessed be He; for we were commanded to know Him and to investigate His unity, and not to rely on tradition (kabbalah) alone. And this knowledge is attained through His actions and His wondrous deeds, and His upper and lower creations." "<b>And behold,</b> this knowledge is the possible one; but knowledge from the side of His essence and reality is impossible, and one cannot grasp it. And concerning this it is said (Proverbs 25:2): ״כבוד אלהים הסתר דבר״. And because the subject of divinity is not something that a person grasps with his intellect at the outset of his thought, therefore the Torah employed the expression והשבות אל לבבך — 'and you shall bring back to your heart' — like a person who contemplates a matter and needs to go back and contemplate it again, in the manner that we find with Elijah (I Kings 19:11–12): ״לא ברוח ה'״, and after the wind an earthquake — ״לא ברעש ה'״; and after the earthquake fire — ״לא באש ה'״; and after the fire ״קול דממה דקה״. The meaning of the verse is that when a person thinks about Him, what He is — whether He is wind or earthquake or fire — he will turn back and refute the construction of his thought regarding whatever he thinks about Him, and after all the thoughts he will find nothing but concealment and cessation, and this is ״ואחר האש קול דממה דקה״ — in the manner mentioned in the Book of Formation: ״בלום פיך מלדבר ולבך מלהרהר״. And it says לבבך and not לבך, for there are two inclinations in a person — the good inclination and the evil inclination — and it comes to teach that if his inclination presses him to turn aside from the path of faith, God forbid, and to believe in two ruling powers, then he shall bring back to his heart that He is one in heaven and on earth; and therefore it concludes: ״אין עוד״. And the subject of bringing back the heart is a matter that requires great examination and surpassing understanding, from what the prophet, peace be upon him, said (Isaiah 44:19): ״ולא ישיב אל לבו לא דעת ולא תבונה לאמר״ — and the matter is that he should reckon in his heart that YHWH is Elohim, the Guide of the world: in heaven — these are the spheres; on high — these are the angels; and upon the earth — the lower world; below — the depths of the abysses. And all of this is included in the language of Scripture that Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 25:3): ״שמים לרום וארץ לעומק״; and it says ״אין עוד״ — in all the worlds, all of them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:46.912131970Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}